Descent lemma, proof (1 of 2)
Write \(v = y - x\) for the step, so that \(y = x - \gamma\nabla f(x)\) means \(v = -\gamma\nabla f(x)\), and \(x_t = x + tv\) for the point a fraction \(t\) of the way along it, so that \(\|x_t - x\| = t\|v\|\)
\[
\begin{alignedat}{2}
f(y) - f(x) &= \int_0^1 \nabla f(x_t)^T v\, dt
&\quad& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(a) along the step}} \\
&\class{fragment}{{}= \nabla f(x)^T v + \int_0^1 \big[\nabla f(x_t) - \nabla f(x)\big]^T v\, dt}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(b) add and subtract}} \\
&\class{fragment}{{}\le \nabla f(x)^T v + \int_0^1 \|\nabla f(x_t) - \nabla f(x)\|\,\|v\|\, dt}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(c) Cauchy-Schwarz}} \\
&\class{fragment}{{}\le \nabla f(x)^T v + \int_0^1 L\,t\,\|v\|^2\, dt}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(d) Lipschitz}}
\end{alignedat}
\]

Descent lemma, proof (2 of 2)
\[
\begin{alignedat}{2}
f(y) - f(x) &\le \nabla f(x)^T v + \int_0^1 L\,t\,\|v\|^2\, dt
&\quad& \\
&\class{fragment}{{}= \nabla f(x)^T v + \tfrac{L}{2}\|v\|^2}
&& \class{fragment}{{\color{gray}\small\text{(e) } \textstyle\int_0^1 t\, dt = \tfrac12}} \\
&\class{fragment}{{}= -\gamma\|\nabla f(x)\|^2 + \tfrac{L}{2}\gamma^2\|\nabla f(x)\|^2}
&& \class{fragment}{{\color{gray}\small\text{(f) } v = -\gamma\nabla f(x)}} \\
&\class{fragment}{{}= -\gamma\big(1 - \tfrac12 L\gamma\big)\|\nabla f(x)\|^2}
&& \class{fragment}{{\color{gray}\small\text{(g) collect}}} \\[1.2em]
&\class{fragment}{\rlap{\text{At } \gamma = 1/L\text{: } f(y) - f(x) \le -\|\nabla f(x)\|^2/2L\text{, the lemma. } \blacksquare}}
&&
\end{alignedat}
\]

Inverse propensity weighting theorem, proof (1 of 2)
Start from what we want, and change one term at a time
\[
\begin{alignedat}{2}
\mathbb E[Y \mid do(T = 1)]
&= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_Y}\big[\,\underbrace{g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)}_{\text{(a)}}\,\big]
&\quad& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(a) } Y \text{ under } do(T = 1)} \\[4pt]
&\class{fragment}{{}= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_Y}\Big[\,\underbrace{\frac{\pi(Z)}{\pi(Z)}}_{\text{(b)}}\, g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)\Big]}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(b) } {=}\ 1\text{, as } \pi(Z) &gt; 0} \\[4pt]
&\class{fragment}{{}= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_Y}\Big[\,\frac{\overbrace{\mathbb E_{\varepsilon_T}[T \mid Z, \varepsilon_Y]}^{\text{(c)}}}{\pi(Z)}\, g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)\Big]}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(c) } {=}\ \pi(Z)\text{: independence}}
\end{alignedat}
\]

Inverse propensity weighting theorem, proof (2 of 2)
\[
\begin{alignedat}{2}
\mathbb E[Y \mid do(T = 1)]
&= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_Y}\Big[\,\frac{\mathbb E_{\varepsilon_T}[T \mid Z, \varepsilon_Y]}{\pi(Z)}\, g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)\Big]
&\quad& \\[4pt]
&\class{fragment}{{}= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_Y}\Big[\,\underbrace{\mathbb E_{\varepsilon_T}\Big[\frac{T\, g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)}{\pi(Z)} \;\Big|\; Z, \varepsilon_Y\Big]}_{\text{(d)}}\Big]}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(d) factor moved inside}} \\[4pt]
&\class{fragment}{{}= \mathbb E_{Z, \varepsilon_T, \varepsilon_Y}\Big[\frac{T\, g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y)}{\pi(Z)}\Big]}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(e) tower property}} \\[4pt]
&\class{fragment}{{}= \mathbb E_{T, Z, Y}\Big[\frac{T\,Y}{\pi(Z)}\Big] \quad \blacksquare}
&& \class{fragment}{\color{gray}\small\text{(f) } T\,g(1, Z, \varepsilon_Y) = T\,Y}
\end{alignedat}
\]
For \(T = 0\): the same steps with \(1 - T\) and \(1 - \pi(Z)\)

Bias–variance decomposition
\(y_i = f^\star(x_i) + \varepsilon_i\), inputs fixed, noise independent, mean \(0\), variance \(\sigma^2\); new \(Y_0 = f^\star(x_0) + \varepsilon_0\).
Bias–variance decomposition. \[
\mathbb E\big[(Y_0 - \hat f_k(x_0))^2\big]
= \underbrace{\sigma^2}_{\text{noise}}
+ \underbrace{\Big[f^\star(x_0) - \tfrac{1}{k}\textstyle\sum_{l=1}^k f^\star(x_{(l)})\Big]^2}_{\text{bias}^2}
+ \underbrace{\frac{\sigma^2}{k}}_{\text{variance}}
\]

noise + bias² + variance. Variance falls in \(k\), always. Bias typically grows too, but it need not be monotonic (a constant \(f^\star\) keeps it at zero for every \(k\)).

